{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794398586,"nonce":"9+hTz7JOtBql5yWQhTgnRNUlT+s=","message":"Correction to the comment above. Its first reason for not storing a closure was wrong: unrooting from a finalizer does not deadlock. Stock HashLink's GC lock is recursive, and an experiment shows it working.\n\nThe experiment (scratch copy, not in the repository): a handle whose value roots a Haxe function and unroots it when dropped, 200 kept and 10,000 discarded over five forced collections.\n\n- Stock HashLink 1.15.0: all 10,000 discarded ones finalized and unrooted, no crash; the 200 kept still call correctly.\n- Ash `--mode jit`: the same.\n- hxcpp 4.3.2 (`hx::GCAddRoot`, removed from the finalizer): the same, one collection behind.\n- Ash default (hybrid) and `--mode interp`: none of the 10,000 were ever finalized, though the closures were made in a frame of their own that holds nothing else. Plain handles are finalized in those modes, so something a closure keeps alive there reaches the handle.\n\nWhat does stand, and is the real problem: a root in native code is invisible to the collector, so a function that can reach the handle rooting it is never collected. 1,000 of 1,000 such pairs survived two collections on stock HashLink, on Ash and on hxcpp. Event handlers usually can reach their emitter.\n\nSo a stored closure cannot rely on the collector to be released. The safe forms are: an explicit close on the Haxe thread, with the finalizer only as a backstop; keeping the function in a field of the Haxe object and lending it for each call, so every reference is one the collector can see; or the stream, which holds nothing of the host's. The second reason given above is unchanged: a worker thread cannot call into the VM.","files":null}]}